    NAME
        MUIA_NList_TitleSeparator -- [ISG], BOOL

    FUNCTION
        If you set it, you get an horizontal bar between the
        title and the first visible entry (only when some title
        is visible).

    DEFAULT
        TRUE

    SEE ALSO
        MUIA_NList_Title
NList.mcc/MUIA_NList_TypeSelect

    NAME
        MUIA_NList_TypeSelect -- Choose line or character-range selection.

    INPUTS
        Initialize/set; LONG. MUIV_NList_TypeSelect_Line (0, default) or
        Char (1). No local getter.

    FUNCTION
        Line mode uses per-row selection flags and an active entry. Char
        mode uses text-range endpoints and supports partial displayed text,
        including wrapped rows. It does not establish ordinary Active
        semantics.

        Changing the mode clears Active and attempts to reset both selection
        representations. The line-clearing calls pass FALSE as the exception
        index, so row zero is skipped; do not assume every old line flag is
        cleared. Character reset is also suppressed while scrollbar updates
        are in progress. Use the public modes; internal selection variants
        are not additional declared inputs.

    SEE ALSO
        MUIM_NList_Select, MUIM_NList_GetSelectInfo, MUIM_NList_NextSelected

NList.mcc/MUIA_NList_Visible

    NAME
        MUIA_NList_Visible -- Read the current visible-row count.

    INPUTS
        Get; LONG. MUIA_List_Visible is an alias.

    FUNCTION
        Return the stored visible-row count while SHOW is true; return -1
        while hidden. The count describes display rows, including wrapping,
        and is not the total entry count. Use it after layout. It is an
        internal geometry value, not an application-settable count.

        The local setter consumes this own tag and suppresses some
        subsequent work in that tag list; do not set it as a way to resize
        or scroll the list.

    SEE ALSO
        MUIA_NList_First, MUIA_NList_Entries

NList.mcc/MUIA_NList_XXXBackground

    NAME
        MUIA_NList_TitleBackground -- [ISG], LONG
        MUIA_NList_ListBackground -- [ISG], LONG
        MUIA_NList_SelectBackground -- [ISG], LONG
        MUIA_NList_CursorBackground -- [ISG], LONG
        MUIA_NList_UnselCurBackground -- [ISG], LONG

    FUNCTION
        You can set all backgroungs of NList with these attributes,
        look at Area.mui/MUIA_Background and Image.mui/MUIA_Image_Spec
        to see what kind of value can be used.
        Anyway, you can set the defaults with prefs and so should
        not set it yourself.
NList.mcc/MUIA_NList_XXXPen

    NAME
        MUIA_NList_TitlePen -- [ISG], LONG
        MUIA_NList_ListPen -- [ISG], LONG
        MUIA_NList_SelectPen -- [ISG], LONG
        MUIA_NList_CursorPen -- [ISG], LONG
        MUIA_NList_UnselCurPen -- [ISG], LONG

    FUNCTION
        You can set all pens of NList with these attributes, their
        value must be of the type Pendisplay.mui/MUIA_Pendisplay_Spec.
        Look at Pendisplay.mui, Area.mui/MUIA_Background and
        Image.mui/MUIA_Image_Spec.

        Anyway, you can set the defaults with prefs and so should
        not set it yourself.
NList.mcc/MUIM_NList_Clear

    NAME
        MUIM_NList_Clear -- Remove all logical entries and display rows.

    SYNOPSIS
        DoMethod(obj, MUIM_NList_Clear);

    FUNCTION
        Destruct each logical payload once, release the display records and
        entry array, and reset the active entry to Off, First to zero and
        InsertPosition to -1. Borrowed payloads are not freed when no
        destructor is installed. All list-owned payload pointers become
        invalid.

        Request layout, wrapping, scrollbar and redraw updates. Visible is
        reset only when the list is not quiet; quiet mode retains it until
        recalculation. Clearing an already empty list is allowed. Destructor
        results are ignored.

    RESULT
        Always TRUE.

    SEE ALSO
        MUIM_NList_Remove, MUIM_NList_Destruct

NList.mcc/MUIM_NList_ColWidth

    NAME
        MUIM_NList_ColWidth --

    SYNOPSIS
        DoMethod(obj,MUIM_NList_ColWidth,LONG col,LONG width);

    FUNCTION
        Set a width for a col as if changed by the user with mouse, or
        reset it to its default size.
        Permit to get the user width of a col too.

    INPUTS
        col     number of the col (numbered as in display hook).

                special value :
              MUIV_NList_ColWidth_All     will set it for all columns.

        width   width to set (4 is the minimum width accepted).

                special values :
              MUIV_NList_ColWidth_Default reset to default.
              MUIV_NList_ColWidth_Get     set nothing, only return current.

    RESULT
        When a col is specified (ie not MUIV_NList_ColWidth_All), the current
        *user* width of the col will be returned :
        o -1 mean that the col has its default width, ie not set by the user
          or with this method.
        o 0  mean that the specified col is not valid.
          When MUIV_NList_ColWidth_All you'll get always 0.
NList.mcc/MUIM_NList_ColToColumn

    NAME
        MUIM_NList_ColToColumn --

    SYNOPSIS
        DoMethod(obj,MUIM_NList_ColToColumn,LONG col);

    FUNCTION
        converts display hook col number to visible column number.

    INPUTS
        col     number of the col (numbered as in display hook).

    RESULT
        column number, -1 if no column use that col number.

    SEE ALSO
        MUIM_NList_ColumnToCol, MUIM_NList_SetColumnCol, MUIA_NList_Columns
NList.mcc/MUIM_NList_ColumnToCol

    NAME
        MUIM_NList_ColumnToCol --

    SYNOPSIS
        DoMethod(obj,MUIM_NList_ColumnToCol,LONG column);

    FUNCTION
        converts visible column number to display hook col number.

    INPUTS
        column   number of the column (visible).

    RESULT
        col number, -1 if the column is not valid.

    SEE ALSO
        MUIM_NList_ColToColumn, MUIM_NList_SetColumnCol, MUIA_NList_Columns
NList.mcc/MUIM_NList_Compare

    NAME
        MUIM_NList_Compare -- Compare two logical payloads.

    SYNOPSIS
        Subclass handler: struct MUIP_NList_Compare *msg;

    INPUTS
        entry1, entry2 -- Borrowed payloads. sort_type1, sort_type2 -- LONG
        comparison-policy values.

    FUNCTION
        Invoke CompareHook or CompareHook2 when installed; otherwise call
        Stricmp on the two payloads. The default requires readable NUL-
        terminated strings and ignores sort types. The legacy hook receives
        entry2 as object and entry1 as message; Hook2 receives the NList
        object and the structured arguments.

        Sorting calls this extension point. A subclass that delegates must
        preserve the original message. A custom comparator must provide
        consistent ordering for the retained payload type.

    RESULT
        Signed negative, zero or positive comparison result carried in IPTR.
        The default does not restrict the magnitude to -1 or 1. Sort breaks
        equal comparisons by prior entry position, retaining equal-entry
        order.

    SEE ALSO
        MUIA_NList_CompareHook, MUIA_NList_CompareHook2, MUIM_NList_Sort

NList.mcc/MUIM_NList_Construct

    NAME
        MUIM_NList_Construct -- Construct a logical entry payload.

    SYNOPSIS
        Subclass handler: struct MUIP_NList_Construct *msg;

    INPUTS
        entry -- Non-NULL input payload for construction. pool -- Pool
        supplied by NList. The message owns neither.

    FUNCTION
        NList calls this extension point during insertion and replacement.
        With a configured hook, dispatch using the selected legacy or
        message convention. Without a hook, return the original entry
        unchanged. NULL input returns NULL without calling the hook.

        A subclass may override this method with a matching Destruct policy.
        If delegating, pass the original message to the superclass.
        Application code should insert entries through Insert or
        ReplaceSingle instead of calling this lifecycle method directly.

    RESULT
        Retained payload pointer or NULL. NULL is a construction failure;
        ReplaceSingle then removes the old entry. Insertion may retain other
        successfully constructed entries.

    SEE ALSO
        MUIA_NList_ConstructHook, MUIA_NList_ConstructHook2,
        MUIM_NList_Destruct

NList.mcc/MUIM_NList_ContextMenuBuild

    NAME
        MUIM_NList_ContextMenuBuild --

    SYNOPSIS
        DoMethod(obj,MUIM_NList_ContextMenuBuild, LONG mx, LONG my, LONG pos,
                                                LONG column, LONG flags,LONG ontop);

    FUNCTION
        Give possibilities to use MUIM_ContextMenuBuild builtin in NList
        and a custom context menu for a NList subclass.

        Here is how NList MUIM_ContextMenuBuild work :

        If MUIA_ContextMenu is NULL, NULL is returned (no menu).

        If MUIA_ContextMenu is none of special values, the supermethod is called.

        If MUIA_ContextMenu is MUIV_NList_ContextMenu_Never, MUIA_ContextMenu is
        set to NULL and NULL is returned.

        Else (it's a special value) MUIM_NList_ContextMenuBuild is called :

          If MUIM_NList_ContextMenuBuild return -1, NULL will be returned (no menu).

          If MUIM_NList_ContextMenuBuild return NULL, NList will return its own
          context menu, depending on prefs (it's the default case).

          If MUIM_NList_ContextMenuBuild return an menu object, NList will
          enable/disable its own menu entries in the menu if it found some,
          then return thqt object.

          2 special menuitems are reconized by NList, and are found by :
            DoMethod(MenuObj,MUIM_FindUData,MUIV_NList_Menu_Default_This)
          and
            DoMethod(MenuObj,MUIM_FindUData,MUIV_NList_Menu_Default_All)



        MUIA_ContextMenu special values are actuallly used :

          MUIV_NList_ContextMenu_Default   replaced by one of followings.
          MUIV_NList_ContextMenu_TopOnly   only on title/top of list.
          MUIV_NList_ContextMenu_Always    always.
          MUIV_NList_ContextMenu_Never     never, repaced by NULL after.

          when using MUIV_NList_ContextMenu_TopOnly NList will set MUIA_ContextMenu
          NULL/non-NULL when the mouse move, which should permit to have
          the window menu avaible when the contents menu is not visible.

          Anyway actually (MUI 3.8) MUI has a bug/feature which make the
          MUIA_ContextMenu being looked at only when the mouse *enter*
          within the object bounds. MUIV_NList_ContextMenu_TopOnly stuff
          will be nicer when that MUI problem is fixed...

        Of course if you want NList to do what has to be done when a menu item
        has been selected, your MUIM_ContextMenuChoice should call the supermethod.

    INPUTS (for MUIM_NList_ContextMenuBuild)

        mx      current mouse x.
        my      current mouse y.
        pos     entry number returned by NList_TestPos.
        column  column returned by NList_TestPos.
        flags   flags returned by NList_TestPos.
        ontop   TRUE if mouse is on title/top of the list.

    RESULT
        NULL, -1 or a menustrip object.

    EXEMPLES
        To make a ContextMenu but have the NList one appear when the
        mouse in on title/top, just set MUIA_ContextMenu,MUIV_NList_ContextMenu_Always
        and make MUIM_NList_ContextMenuBuild return NULL when ontop in TRUE (else
        return your menustrip object). Call MUIM_ContextMenuChoice supermethod too.

        To make your own context menu which have the same menuitems as ones
        in NList context menu, set MUIA_ContextMenu,MUIV_NList_ContextMenu_Always
        and make MUIM_NList_ContextMenuBuild return always your menustrip object.
        Make 4 menuitems in your menustrip object which will be like these :
        { NM_ITEM ,  "Default Width: this" , 0 ,0 ,0 ,(APTR) MUIV_NList_Menu_DefWidth_This },
        { NM_ITEM ,  "Default Width: all"  , 0 ,0 ,0 ,(APTR) MUIV_NList_Menu_DefWidth_All },
        { NM_ITEM ,  "Default Order: this" , 0 ,0 ,0 ,(APTR) MUIV_NList_Menu_DefOrder_This },
        { NM_ITEM ,  "Default Order: all"  , 0 ,0 ,0 ,(APTR) MUIV_NList_Menu_DefOrder_All },
        They will be automatically enabled/disabled as needed by NList  :)
        Call MUIM_ContextMenuChoice supermethod too.
NList.mcc/MUIM_NList_CopyTo

    NAME
        MUIM_NList_CopyTo --

    SYNOPSIS
        DoMethod(obj,MUIM_NList_CopyTo, LONG pos, char *filename,
                                        APTR *result, APTR *entries);

    FUNCTION
        Do a copy to clipboard from some list entries or strings.

    INPUTS
        pos      entry number to be copied.
                 if MUIA_NList_CopyToClipHook is specified, it's what
                 it will return which will be copied instead of just
                 using the entry pointer as a string pointer.

                 special values :

            MUIV_NList_CopyTo_Active    copy list active entry
            MUIV_NList_CopyTo_Selected  copy list selected entries
            MUIV_NList_CopyTo_All       copy all list entries
            MUIV_NList_CopyTo_Entries   copy specified entries
            MUIV_NList_CopyTo_Entry     copy specified entry

            a "\n" will be insert after each entry contents.

        filename name of the file to copy to. If NULL then the copy will be
                 done to a string (strptr).
        result   LONG pointer which fill be filled with MUIV_NLCT_Success if
                 no error occured while opening and writing in the file, else
                 it will be filled with MUIV_NLCT_OpenErr, MUIV_NLCT_WriteErr
                 or MUIV_NLCT_Failed (failed somewhere when making copy data).

                 if filename is NULL, result will be filled with a string pointer
                 to a string allocated by AllocVec(). Datas will have been copied
                 in that string. YOU'LL HAVE TO FREE TAHT string pointer YOURSELF,
                 with a FreeVec(). If the returned string pointer is NULL then
                 the copy failed.
        entries  pointer to some entry, string, entries array or string array.
                 Its use depend on the pos value :
                 if MUIV_NList_CopyTo_Entry   then it's an entry pointer.
                 if MUIV_NList_CopyTo_Entries then it's an entry pointer
                                              array NULL terminated.
                 else :  not used, set to NULL.

    EXEMPLES
        LONG result = 0;
        DoMethod(obj,MUIM_NList_CopyTo, 5, "RAM:test.txt", &result, NULL, NULL);

          will copy the 5th entry to file RAM:test.txt, using
          MUIA_NList_CopyToClipHook if set.


        LONG result = 0;
        DoMethod(obj,MUIM_NList_CopyTo, MUIV_NList_CopyToClip_Selected,
                                            "PRT:", &result, NULL, NULL);
          will copy all selected entries to printer, using
          MUIA_NList_CopyToClipHook for each if set.


        char *strptr = NULL;
        DoMethod(obj,MUIM_NList_CopyTo, MUIV_NList_CopyTo_All,
                                            NULL, &strptr, NULL, NULL);
          will copy all list entries to the string returned in strptr.
          you must make a FreeVec(strptr) by yourself after.

    SEE ALSO
        MUIM_NList_CopyToClip, MUIA_NList_CopyToClipHook
NList.mcc/MUIM_NList_CopyToClip

    NAME
        MUIM_NList_CopyToClip --

    SYNOPSIS
        DoMethod(obj,MUIM_NList_CopyToClip, LONG pos, ULONG clipnum,
                                            APTR *entries, struct Hook *hook);

    FUNCTION
        Do a copy to clipboard from some list entries or strings.

    INPUTS
        pos      entry number to be copied.
                 if MUIA_NList_CopyToClipHook is specified, it's what
                 it will return which will be copied instead of just
                 using the entry pointer as a string pointer.

                 special values :

            MUIV_NList_CopyToClip_Active    copy list active entry
            MUIV_NList_CopyToClip_Selected  copy list selected entries
            MUIV_NList_CopyToClip_All       copy all list entries
            MUIV_NList_CopyToClip_Entries   copy specified entries
            MUIV_NList_CopyToClip_Entry     copy specified entry
            MUIV_NList_CopyToClip_Strings   copy specified strings using hook
            MUIV_NList_CopyToClip_String    copy specified string using hook

            a "\n" will be insert after each entry contents, for all but
            MUIV_NList_CopyToClip_Strings and MUIV_NList_CopyToClip_String.

        clipnum  clipboard number to copy to.
        entries  pointer to some entry, string, entries array or string array.
                 Its use depend on the pos value :
                 if MUIV_NList_CopyToClip_Entry   then it's an entry pointer.
                 if MUIV_NList_CopyToClip_Entries then it's an entry pointer
                                                  array NULL terminated.
                 if MUIV_NList_CopyToClip_String  then it's a string pointer.
                 if MUIV_NList_CopyToClip_Strings then it's an string pointer
                                                  array NULL terminated.
                 else :  not used, set to NULL.
        hook     hook function which will be used (if not NULL) for
                 MUIV_NList_CopyToClip_Strings and
                 MUIV_NList_CopyToClip_String
                 instead of MUIA_NList_CopyToClipHook
                 Should be NULL most of time.

    EXEMPLES
        DoMethod(obj,MUIM_NList_CopyToClip, 5, 0, NULL, NULL);
          will copy the 5th entry to clipboard 0, using
          MUIA_NList_CopyToClipHook if set.

        DoMethod(obj,MUIM_NList_CopyToClip, MUIV_NList_CopyToClip_Selected,
                                            0, NULL, NULL);
          will copy all selected entries to clipboard 0, using
          MUIA_NList_CopyToClipHook for each if set.

        DoMethod(obj,MUIM_NList_CopyToClip, MUIV_NList_CopyToClip_String,
                                            0, "my string", NULL);
          will copy "my string" to clipboard 0.

    NOTE
        MUIV_NList_CopyToClip_Strings and MUIV_NList_CopyToClip_String
        are here to permit simple text copy to clipboard for non nlist
        object related stuff (anyway a NList object must be here to use
        them...). They can use their own hook instead of nlist one if
        non NULL, anyway look at MUIA_NList_CopyToClipHook to see how
        this hook will be used.

    SEE ALSO
        MUIA_NList_CopyToClipHook, MUIA_NList_CopyToClip_Key, MUIM_NList_CopyTo
NList.mcc/MUIM_NList_CreateImage

    NAME
        MUIM_NList_CreateImage --

    SYNOPSIS
        DoMethod(obj,MUIM_NList_CreateImage,Object *imgobj, ULONG flags);

    FUNCTION
        Same function as List.mui/MUIM_List_CreateImage.

        Any transparent color in the source Bitmap/Bodychunk
        object will work (except if flags is ~0L).

        You must use MUIM_NList_CreateImage in Setup() or after
        and MUIM_NList_DeleteImage in Cleanup() or before,
        because the mri of the NList object must be valid.
        Setup() and Cleanup() are the best because MUI_ObtainPen()
        and MUI_ReleasePen() are used, and then pens will be
        released while iconification and will be re-obtained
        if the screen change.

        Take a look at MUIM_NList_UseImage which is far easier to
        use.

        Standard flags value is 0.

        If flags is ~0L then FillArea will not be set to FALSE on the object
        (for any other value, FillArea is set to FALSE)

        If flags is ~0L for a Bitmap/Bodychunk then it will be really the
        given object which will be drawn, as for non Bitmap/Bodychunk objects.


        The imgobj can (nearly) be any object, but take care of that :

        - the object should not test and use user inputs, the object
          should just be able to draw itself within the _left(obj),
          _top(obj), _width(obj) and height(obj) it will have when its
          Draw method is called.
        - the object class MUST NOT be Group or subclass of it.
        - the given imgobj must not be attached to any group object
          anywhere else, because a ADDMEMBER will be done with it.
        - Each imgobj can be used with one and only one NList object.
        - you can use the return pointer with ESC O[<ptr>] and ESC o[<num>].
        - the object will receive two tags just before its Draw method will
          be called : set(imgobj,MUIA_NLIMG_EntryCurrent,current_entry_number) and
          set(imgobj,MUIA_NLIMG_EntryHeight,height_of_each_entry_of_the_list)
          which are usefull in some case to draw things (see demo example).
        - a new stuff is to use it with ESC O[<ptr>;<tag>;<value>], in that case
          a set(imgobj,tag,value) will be done just before drawing it, so
          you can make an object which will draw different things when that tag
          value is changed. Setting this tag MUST NOT make the object being
          redrawn !!!
          <tag> and <value> must both be in hexadecimal (%lx).
        - if you use ;<tag>;<value> in one ESC sequence for an imgobj, you should
          use it everywhere you use that imgobj because there is no default for it.
        - The imgobj height will always be set to the entries height.
        - The mindefheight of imgobj will become the min height for entries.
        - Think it's still a new testing stuff...

        Note:
          Previously the object was disposed by NList at its end, actually a call
          to MUIM_NList_DeleteImage will REMMEMBER it, and so you have to dispose
          it yourself after !

        Look the demo program to see a way to use it...


    RESULT
        The result you get is a struct BitMapImage pointer which
        will exist between the MUIM_NList_CreateImage and
        MUIM_NList_DeleteImage, with a valid bitmap, width and
        height for the current screen.

        If you use it for a non Bitmap/Bodychunk object (or with flags
        set to ~0L) then the mask and imgbmp fields of the returned
        struct BitMapImage are not valid, and obtainpens is a pointer to
        the object.

        The only thing you should do with it is to include it in
        \33O[%p]. The result may be NULL in which case NList
        was unable to create the image, but the \33O[] combination
        simply draws nothing when receiving a NULL.

        ATTENTION:
        The returned pointer doesn't give the same structure than
        MUIM_List_CreateImage (in standard List class) would do :
        both are not compatible at all !

    SEE ALSO
        MUIM_NList_DeleteImage, MUIM_NList_UseImage
NList.mcc/MUIM_NList_DeleteImage

    NAME
        MUIM_NList_DeleteImage --

    SYNOPSIS
        DoMethod(obj,MUIM_NList_DeleteImage,APTR listimg);

    FUNCTION
        Same function as List.mui/MUIM_List_DeleteImage.

        Delete the image pointer returned from MUIM_NList_CreateImage.
        Read explains in MUIM_NList_CreateImage.

    SEE ALSO
        MUIM_NList_CreateImage, MUIM_NList_UseImage
NList.mcc/MUIM_NList_Destruct

    NAME
        MUIM_NList_Destruct -- Release a logical entry payload.

    SYNOPSIS
        Subclass handler: struct MUIP_NList_Destruct *msg;

    INPUTS
        entry -- Payload returned by Construct. pool -- Corresponding pool.

    FUNCTION
        NList calls this extension point for removal, replacement, clear and
        disposal. If entry and the configured hook are non-NULL, invoke that
        hook. Without a hook no payload storage is freed. Wrapped
        continuation rows do not trigger separate destruction.

        Override this method with the matching Construct policy. Do not free
        borrowed caller storage. When delegating, preserve the original
        message. Application code should use the list lifecycle operations
        instead of invoking Destruct on a live entry.

    RESULT
        Always zero in the default handler; hook return values are ignored.

    SEE ALSO
        MUIM_NList_Construct, MUIA_NList_DestructHook,
        MUIA_NList_DestructHook2

NList.mcc/MUIM_NList_Display

	NAME
		MUIM_NList_Display --

	SYNOPSIS
		DoMethod(obj,MUIM_NList_Display,APTR entry,ULONG entry_pos,
		    STRPTR *strings,STRPTR *preparses);

    FUNCTION
        This method is called  ONLY  by  NList.mcc  when  application  is
        displaying  entry  f.e.  when  MUI_Redraw(), MUIM_NList_Redraw or
        MUIM_NList_RedrawEntry are called. So, this method is only useful
        within  NList.mcc  subclass,  function  method  should looks like
        normal  MUIA_NList_DisplayHook  or  MUIA_NList_DisplayHook2  hook
        function.   If  your  subclass  will  not  override  this  method
        NList.mcc  implementation  will   be   used,   and   it'll   call
        MUIA_NList_DisplayHook  or  MUIA_NList_DisplayHook2  hook in that
        case.

    INPUTS
		entry - Entry  pointer  returned  by  f.e.  MUIM_NList_Construct,
				MUIA_NList_ConstructHook or MUIA_NList_ConstructHook2
				hooks.

		entry_pos - Entry position.

		strings   - Pointer to strings table.

		preparses - Pointer to preparse strings table.

    RESULT
        Currently undefined. If you are overriding it completely,  please
        return 0, if not use the value returned by super class!

	NOTE
        Do not even think about calling it by yourself! Method parameters
        structure might grow in the future! Be warned.

        Internal implementation of this method can use internal or passed
        arguments,  so  please  do  not  cheating  and  if  you  are  not
		overriding it, pass the original arguments to super class.

	SEE ALSO
		MUIA_NList_ConstructHook, MUIA_NList_ConstructHook2,
		MUIA_NList_DisplayHook, MUIA_NList_DisplayHook2,
		MUIM_NList_Insert, MUIM_NList_InsertSingle, MUIM_NList_Replace,
		MUIM_NList_Construct, MUIM_NList_Destruct, MUIM_NList_Compare

NList.mcc/MUIM_NList_DoMethod

    NAME
        MUIM_NList_DoMethod --

    SYNOPSIS
        DoMethod(obj,MUIM_NList_DoMethod,LONG pos,
                      APTR DestObj,ULONG FollowParams,ULONG method,...);

    FUNCTION
        The given method will be executed for each selected entry, the
        active entry, all entries, or one specified entry of the NList
        object.

        Each DoMethod() can be done on the NList object, its _app object,
        a specified object, or the entry pointer if (and only if) it's
        an object.

        Following arguments can be automatically adjusted for each call
        using special values, like in the MUIM_Notify method (you must
        specified the numer of following argument too, as there will be
        copied in a temporary buffer - alloced in the stack).

    INPUTS
        pos -           the entry number for which you want to do the method.

          special values :
          MUIV_NList_DoMethod_Active    do it for the active entry.
          MUIV_NList_DoMethod_Selected  do it for all selected entries.
          MUIV_NList_DoMethod_All       do it

        DestObj -       the object on which the method will be done.

          special values :
          MUIV_NList_DoMethod_Entry     use entry pointer as target object for method.
          MUIV_NList_DoMethod_Self      use the NList object as target for method.
          MUIV_NList_DoMethod_App       use the _app(NList object) as target for method.

        FollowParams -  the number of following parameters (including "method").
                        (maximum 40 to prevent errors and stack overflow)

        method -        the method which will be done.

        ... -           the method parameters.

          special values :
          MUIV_NList_EntryValue         replaced by the "current" entry pointer.
          MUIV_NList_EntryPosValue      replaced by the "current" entry number.
          MUIV_NList_SelfValue          replaced by the NList object.
          MUIV_NList_AppValue           replaced by _app(NList object)

    NOTES
        Don't use this to do things on the NList object when a specific way
        to do it exists (don't use it to remove entries in the nlist object
        itself for example).

        When using it in a notification, MUIV_TriggerValue and MUIV_TriggerValue
        special values will be replaced by the notify superclass as usual.

    EXAMPLES
        Insert all entries from nlist L1 to nlist L2 :

        DoMethod(L1, MUIM_NList_DoMethod, MUIV_NList_DoMethod_All,
          L2, 3,
          MUIM_NList_InsertSingle, MUIV_NList_EntryValue, MUIV_NList_Insert_Bottom);

        (it would be better to make set(L2,MUIA_NList_Quiet,TRUE) before and
         set(L2,MUIA_NList_Quiet,FALSE) after when there are many entries...)


        If the entries of nlist L1 are objects (and only in that case), you can do
        that to call a method (MUIM_Foo,x) for each selected of them :

        DoMethod(L1, MUIM_NList_DoMethod, MUIV_NList_DoMethod_Selected,
          MUIV_NList_DoMethod_Entry, 2,
          MUIM_Foo,x);
NList.mcc/MUIM_NList_DropDraw

    NAME
        MUIM_NList_DropDraw --

    SYNOPSIS
        DoMethod(obj,MUIM_NList_DropDraw, LONG pos, LONG type,
                                          LONG minx,LONG maxx,LONG miny,LONG maxy);

    FUNCTION
        This method MUST NOT be called directly !

        It will be called by NList, and will draw the drop mark previously fixed
        (pos and type) by MUIM_NList_DropType within the minx, maxx, miny, maxy
        in the _rp(obj) rasport. You must not draw outside of the given box,
        because only the corresponding NList entry will be refreshed to erase what
        is drawn in that method.

        Calling the supermethod (so the builtin MUIM_NList_DropDraw method) will draw
        the standard dropmark specified by (type & MUIV_NList_DropType_Mask), so
        MUIV_NList_DropType_None,MUIV_NList_DropType_Above,MUIV_NList_DropType_Below
        or MUIV_NList_DropType_Onto.

        You can draw directly in the rastport (or a clone of it) in that method,
        because it will be called from the Draw method within the DragReport in
        Refresh mode.

    SEE ALSO
        MUIM_NList_DropType, MUIA_NList_DropType, MUIA_NList_DropMark,
        MUIM_NList_DropEntryDrawErase
NList.mcc/MUIM_NList_DropEntryDrawErase

    NAME
        MUIM_NList_DropEntryDrawErase --

    SYNOPSIS
        DoMethod(obj,MUIM_NList_DropEntryDrawErase,LONG type,LONG drawpos,LONG erasepos);

    FUNCTION
        This method MUST NOT be called directly !

        It will be called by NList while the DragReport just before the redraw
        which will draw a new mark or erase the old one.

        This method can be used to change something so your displayhook will return
        something different for the marked entry, like changing its color or making
        it bold or italic.

        Don't call the superclass unless you know that the superclass use it.

        You should return 0.

    INPUTS
        type has the same meaning as *type in MUIM_NList_DropType, it will be useless
        in most cases (and has no meanning at all for erasepos).

        drawpos is the entry number which will be draw with a dropmark, -1 mean none.

        erasepos is the entry number which is not any more the dropmark one, -1 mean none.

    SEE ALSO
        MUIM_NList_DropDraw, MUIA_NList_DropType, MUIA_NList_DropMark
NList.mcc/MUIM_NList_DropType

    NAME
        MUIM_NList_DropType --

    SYNOPSIS
        DoMethod(obj,MUIM_NList_DropType, LONG *pos,LONG *type,
                                          LONG minx,LONG maxx,LONG miny,LONG maxy,
                                          LONG mousex,LONG mousey);

    FUNCTION
        This method MUST NOT be called directly !

        It will be called by NList while the DragReport, with default *pos and *type
        values depending on the drag pointer position that you can modify as you want.

        Default *type can be MUIV_NList_DropType_Above or MUIV_NList_DropType_Below.
        You can change it to any of MUIV_NList_DropType_None,MUIV_NList_DropType_Above,
        MUIV_NList_DropType_Below and MUIV_NList_DropType_Onto if you want, using
        the mouse position and the entry box.

        You can make you own *type value as soon as you don't set it in
        MUIV_NList_DropType_Mask, and so draw what you want depending on that value
        in the MUIM_NList_DropDraw method.

        Note that any MUIV_NList_DropType_Below *type will be changed to
        MUIV_NList_DropType_Above with a *pos incremented by 1 just after the
        return of that method.

        If you change the *pos, the list will be scrolled to see it (if the value is
        correct).

        getting MUIA_NList_DropType or MUIA_NList_DropMark will return the same values
        as *pos and *type.

        If your subclass is a direct NList subclass, then there is no need to call
        the supermethod which done nothing at all !

    SEE ALSO
        MUIM_NList_DropDraw, MUIA_NList_DropType, MUIA_NList_DropMark,
        MUIM_NList_DropEntryDrawErase
NList.mcc/MUIM_NList_Exchange

    NAME
        MUIM_NList_Exchange -- Exchange two logical entries.

    SYNOPSIS
        DoMethod(obj, MUIM_NList_Exchange, pos1, pos2);

    INPUTS
        Both positions accept zero-based display indices or
        MUIV_NList_Exchange_Top (0), Active (-1), Bottom (-2). Only pos2
        also accepts Next (-3) or Previous (-4), relative to the resolved
        first position.

    FUNCTION
        Map wrapped continuations to their logical owners and exchange their
        existing records. No payload is constructed or destructed. The
        active entry follows its record. Clear character selection, mark the
        list unsorted and request rewrapping/redraw. Next can resolve back
        to the same owner when the following display row is a continuation.

    RESULT
        TRUE for two valid, distinct resolved entries. FALSE for invalid or
        identical resolved positions.

    SEE ALSO
        MUIM_NList_Move, MUIM_NList_GetEntry

NList.mcc/MUIM_NList_GetEntry

    NAME
        MUIM_NList_GetEntry -- Retrieve a borrowed logical payload.

    SYNOPSIS
        entry = (APTR)DoMethod(obj, MUIM_NList_GetEntry, pos, &entry);

    INPUTS
        pos -- Zero-based display index or MUIV_NList_GetEntry_Active (-1).
        entry -- Optional writable APTR output storage; NULL omits the
        store.

    FUNCTION
        Map a wrapped continuation row to its logical owner. Return that
        payload, without copying or extracting a displayed substring. The
        optional output receives the same pointer. Removal, replacement,
        clear or disposal may invalidate list-owned payloads; do not free
        them through this borrowed pointer.

    RESULT
        Payload pointer directly, or NULL for an invalid position or no
        active entry. This is not a BOOL success result. MUIM_List_GetEntry
        is routed to this same handler.

    SEE ALSO
        MUIM_NList_GetEntryInfo, MUIM_NList_Construct

NList.mcc/MUIM_NList_GetEntryInfo

    NAME
        MUIM_NList_GetEntryInfo -- Retrieve logical-entry and display-slice
        information.

    SYNOPSIS
        DoMethod(obj, MUIM_NList_GetEntryInfo, &info);

    INPUTS
        info -- Valid writable struct MUI_NList_GetEntryInfo. Initialize it
        before calling.

        pos -- Display-row index, MUIV_NList_GetEntry_Active (-1), or
        MUIV_NList_GetEntryInfo_Line (-2). With Line, supply the zero-based
        logical entry number in line. For ordinary display lookup initialize
        line to zero.

    FUNCTION
        For display lookup, entry_pos identifies the logical owner, entry is
        its borrowed payload, and line is its zero-based logical number.
        wrapcol, charpos and charlen describe the requested display row. For
        Line lookup, the fields describe the owner's first row. pos is not
        rewritten.

        No substring is copied or terminated. Character offsets refer to
        displayed column text; they do not necessarily index an arbitrary
        application payload. Use slice fields only when the wrap state and
        completed layout make them meaningful. Payload ownership is
        unchanged.

        The implementation also recognizes internal modes: pos -3 uses
        InsertPosition, falling back to the last row when that value is -1;
        line -2 in display lookup returns a private entry record and leaves
        the remaining slice fields unchanged. These are not public header
        selectors; application code should use the documented modes and
        initialize line.

    RESULT
        Always TRUE. No match sets entry to NULL, entry_pos and line to -1,
        and the slice fields to zero. Check entry instead of treating TRUE
        as evidence of a match.

    SEE ALSO
        MUIM_NList_GetEntry

NList.mcc/MUIM_NList_GetPos

    NAME
        MUIM_NList_GetPos -- Find the next stored payload pointer match.

    SYNOPSIS
        DoMethod(obj, MUIM_NList_GetPos, entry, &pos);

    INPUTS
        entry -- Borrowed stored payload pointer. pos -- Writable LONG
        cursor, initialized to MUIV_NList_GetPos_Start (-1) or a valid
        previous result.

    FUNCTION
        Search after pos for pointer identity, not content equality. Use the
        pointer retained by Construct. NULL is not found. The scan inspects
        stored display records without normalizing wrapped continuations to
        their owner. No payload is copied or owned by this call.

        The implementation also recognizes entry pointer -2 as an internal
        last-inserted query; it can return TRUE with position -1 in an empty
        list. This is not a declared public selector. Application code
        should use ordinary payload pointers and the public cursor
        constants.

    RESULT
        TRUE and the matching index, or FALSE and MUIV_NList_GetPos_End
        (-1). Stop at End; another call using -1 restarts the search.

    SEE ALSO
        MUIM_NList_GetEntry, MUIM_NList_SetActive

NList.mcc/MUIM_NList_GetSelectInfo

    NAME
        MUIM_NList_GetSelectInfo -- Read selection-range fields.

    SYNOPSIS
        DoMethod(obj, MUIM_NList_GetSelectInfo, &res);

    INPUTS
        res -- Valid writable struct MUI_NList_GetSelectInfo; NULL is not
        accepted.

    FUNCTION
        The structure contains start/end logical-owner row positions,
        vstart/vend display-row positions, vnum, num,
        start_column/end_column and start_pos/end_pos. num is unused and is
        always set to -1. Logical-owner adjustment maps wrapped continuation
        indices back to their first row; it is not conversion to a dense
        logical-entry ordinal.

        In line mode, vnum counts selected display records and end tracks
        the last selected row. However, start is initialized to 1 and the
        scan tests for -1 before assigning it, so the first-selected
        position is not captured. vstart inherits 1; start may then be
        adjusted if row 1 is a continuation. With no selection, end and vend
        also remain 1, while vnum is zero. The previous guarantee of -1
        bounds for no selection is not implemented.

        In character mode, endpoints and column/character positions are
        copied from the selection state. vnum includes both endpoint rows
        when their indices are ordered; it does not subtract a boundary-only
        final row. Invalid endpoints are not fully normalized. Use
        NextSelected for row traversal and inspect these limitations before
        relying on bounds.

    RESULT
        Always TRUE. It does not validate the selection or guarantee correct
        normalized bounds.

    SEE ALSO
        MUIM_NList_NextSelected, MUIM_NList_Select

NList.mcc/MUIM_NList_Insert

    NAME
        MUIM_NList_Insert -- Insert logical entries.

    SYNOPSIS
        DoMethod(obj, MUIM_NList_Insert, entries, count, pos, flags);

    INPUTS
        entries -- APTR * pointer array, or a readable NUL-terminated string
        when count is -2. NULL inserts nothing.

        count -- Positive item count; -1 counts pointers up to the first
        NULL; -2 splits text on LF, CR or CRLF. String mode includes an
        empty final entry after a trailing separator and one empty entry for
        an empty string. Other nonpositive counts insert nothing.

        pos -- Insert before this zero-based display-row index.
        MUIV_NList_Insert_Top (0), Active (-1), Sorted (-2) and Bottom (-3)
        are supported. Active without a valid active entry appends. Other
        out-of-range positions also append. A wrapped continuation position
        is adjusted to its logical owner.

        flags -- ULONG; zero or MUIV_NList_Insert_Flag_Raw. Raw skips per-
        entry column-add processing; it does not bypass construction,
        sorting or all redraw work.

    FUNCTION
        Call Construct for each candidate, retaining non-NULL results.
        Without a construct hook the original payload pointers are borrowed;
        with the string hook each line is copied. Keep uncopied payloads
        alive until their list lifetime ends. Entries are initially
        unwrapped and left-aligned.

        Sorted requests sort the full list or merge into the previously
        sorted list. The current comparison policy must match the ordering
        of an incrementally merged prefix. Character selection is cleared
        and geometry/redraw work is requested as needed.

    RESULT
        TRUE when the insertion path retains entries, FALSE for no retained
        insertion. This is neither an inserted count nor proof that every
        input succeeded. Construct failure can leave a partial insertion.

    NOTES
        This implementation does not provide a complete bounded traversal or
        allocation-failure guarantee for pointer-array insertion, including
        the single-entry wrappers. Do not treat count or a NULL terminator
        as proof that all internal reads stay within the input array.
        Allocation failure is not handled transactionally. These are
        implementation limitations, not additional caller-supplied padding
        requirements.

    SEE ALSO
        MUIM_NList_InsertWrap, MUIM_NList_InsertSingle, MUIM_NList_Construct

NList.mcc/MUIM_NList_InsertSingle

    NAME
        MUIM_NList_InsertSingle -- Insert one logical entry.

    SYNOPSIS
        DoMethod(obj, MUIM_NList_InsertSingle, entry, pos);

    INPUTS
        entry -- Non-NULL input payload. pos -- Insert before this zero-
        based display-row index. MUIV_NList_Insert_Top (0), Active (-1),
        Sorted (-2) and Bottom (-3) are supported. Active without a valid
        active entry appends. Other out-of-range positions also append. A
        wrapped continuation position is adjusted to its logical owner.

    FUNCTION
        Use the common Insert path with count one, NOWRAP, ALIGN_LEFT and
        flags zero. Construction and payload ownership follow Insert. NULL
        entry returns FALSE without insertion. The common pointer traversal
        and failure limitations apply to this wrapper as well.

    RESULT
        Common insertion BOOL, not an entry index.

    SEE ALSO
        MUIM_NList_Insert, MUIM_NList_InsertSingleWrap

NList.mcc/MUIM_NList_InsertSingleWrap

    NAME
        MUIM_NList_InsertSingleWrap -- Insert one entry with wrapping and
        alignment.

    SYNOPSIS
        DoMethod(obj, MUIM_NList_InsertSingleWrap, entry, pos, wrapcol,
        align);

    INPUTS
        entry and pos -- As for InsertSingle.

        wrapcol -- NOWRAP or one of WRAPCOL0 through WRAPCOL6. Only one
        display column can wrap; the implementation masks to these bits and
        selects the lowest set bit if several are supplied.

        align -- ALIGN_LEFT, ALIGN_CENTER, ALIGN_RIGHT or ALIGN_JUSTIFY.
        Stored after masking with 0x0700. Alignment escape sequences in the
        parsed display text can override this initial style.

    FUNCTION
        Delegate to common insertion with count one and flags zero. NULL
        entry returns FALSE. Construct/destruct ownership and the
        traversal/failure limitations of Insert apply unchanged.

    RESULT
        Common insertion BOOL.

    SEE ALSO
        MUIM_NList_Insert, MUIM_NList_InsertWrap, MUIM_NList_ReplaceSingle

NList.mcc/MUIM_NList_InsertWrap

    NAME
        MUIM_NList_InsertWrap -- Insert entries with wrapping and alignment.

    SYNOPSIS
        DoMethod(obj, MUIM_NList_InsertWrap, entries, count, pos, wrapcol,
        align, flags);

    INPUTS
        entries, count, pos and flags -- As for Insert, including the final
        ULONG flags argument.

        wrapcol -- NOWRAP or one of WRAPCOL0 through WRAPCOL6. Only one
        display column can wrap; the implementation masks to these bits and
        selects the lowest set bit if several are supplied.

        align -- ALIGN_LEFT, ALIGN_CENTER, ALIGN_RIGHT or ALIGN_JUSTIFY.
        Stored after masking with 0x0700. Alignment escape sequences in the
        parsed display text can override this initial style.

    FUNCTION
        Use the common Insert path with the supplied wrapping and alignment.
        Wrapping creates display rows that refer to one logical payload; it
        does not create independently owned payloads for each continuation.
        The same input traversal, partial-insertion and allocation-failure
        limitations apply.

    RESULT
        Common insertion BOOL, not a count of logical entries or wrapped
        rows.

    SEE ALSO
        MUIM_NList_Insert, MUIM_NList_InsertSingleWrap,
        MUIM_NList_GetEntryInfo

NList.mcc/MUIM_NList_Jump

    NAME
        MUIM_NList_Jump -- Request that a display row be visible.

    SYNOPSIS
        DoMethod(obj, MUIM_NList_Jump, pos);

    INPUTS
        pos -- Display-row index or MUIV_NList_Jump_Top (0), Active (-1),
        Bottom (-2), Down (-3), Up (-4), Active_Center (-5).

    FUNCTION
        Ordinary requests change First only when a valid row lies outside
        the visible range. Up targets First minus one; Down targets First
        plus Visible. They do not change Active or select entries. Use valid
        completed layout; the calculation uses the stored visible-row count.

        Active_Center assigns a first-row position around Active,
        constrained at the list edges. This branch does not require a valid
        active row and reports work even if First remains unchanged. Invalid
        ordinary requests do nothing.

    RESULT
        Always TRUE from the method wrapper, including invalid or already-
        visible requests. It does not prove that scrolling occurred.

    SEE ALSO
        MUIA_NList_First, MUIA_NList_Visible, MUIM_NList_SetActive

NList.mcc/MUIM_NList_Move

    NAME
        MUIM_NList_Move -- Move one entry or the selected entry group.

    SYNOPSIS
        DoMethod(obj, MUIM_NList_Move, from, to);

    INPUTS
        from -- Zero-based display index or MUIV_NList_Move_Top (0), Active
        (-1), Bottom (-2), Selected (-5).

        to -- A final display position for an ordinary move, or an insertion
        boundary for Selected. Top, Active and Bottom are accepted. Next
        (-3) and Previous (-4) apply only to ordinary moves. Active must
        identify a valid entry.

    FUNCTION
        Ordinary moves normalize wrapped continuations to their owner, move
        the existing record, track the active payload and update
        InsertPosition. No payload is copied or destructed. Invalid and
        identical resolved positions do nothing. When moving downward past a
        wrapped destination, the destination includes that owner's
        continuation rows.

        Selected moves preserve relative order and include the logical
        owners of selected wrapped rows. In character-selection mode they
        use the covered entry range. A numeric destination is interpreted
        before removal of the selected block. The multi-entry path uses a
        temporary pooled array and does not validate a missing Active
        destination. Use a valid destination. This path does not explicitly
        clear the internal sorted flag; explicitly sort again before relying
        on sorted insertion after manual reordering.

    RESULT
        Ordinary move: TRUE for a move, FALSE for no valid change. Selected:
        TRUE can also mean no selection or an unchanged contiguous block;
        the single-entry branch returns the ordinary move result. Temporary
        allocation failure returns FALSE.

    SEE ALSO
        MUIM_NList_Exchange, MUIM_NList_Sort

NList.mcc/MUIM_NList_NextSelected

    NAME
        MUIM_NList_NextSelected -- Iterate forward through selected display
        rows.

    SYNOPSIS
        DoMethod(obj, MUIM_NList_NextSelected, &pos);

    INPUTS
        pos -- Writable LONG cursor. Initialize with
        MUIV_NList_NextSelected_Start (-1). End is also -1.

    FUNCTION
        Advance after the previous row, scanning line-selection flags or the
        character-selection range. Negative input restarts at the beginning.
        Character-range traversal excludes an end row when the endpoint is
        exactly its beginning.

        Each returned index is a display row; wrapped rows can share a
        logical payload. Stop when pos equals End. Calling again with End
        restarts traversal. Do not mutate the list while retaining
        positional iteration state.

    RESULT
        Always TRUE. Read pos to detect exhaustion; it is not a BOOL has-
        next result.

    SEE ALSO
        MUIM_NList_Select, MUIM_NList_GetEntry, MUIM_NList_PrevSelected

NList.mcc/MUIM_NList_PrevSelected

    NAME
        MUIM_NList_PrevSelected -- Iterate backward through selected display
        rows.

    SYNOPSIS
        DoMethod(obj, MUIM_NList_PrevSelected, &pos);

    INPUTS
        pos -- Writable LONG cursor. Initialize with
        MUIV_NList_PrevSelected_Start (-1). End is also -1.

    FUNCTION
        Scan backward before the previous row. Negative or out-of-range
        input starts at the last row. In line mode, the scan stops at row
        zero without testing whether it is selected: zero can be a false
        match. Use forward iteration or verify that row with Select_Ask when
        accurate membership is required.

        In character mode, clamping from above the range can return the end
        row even when the endpoint is at that row's beginning. The reverse
        and forward paths do not normalize that boundary identically.

        Each returned index is a display row; wrapped rows can share a
        logical payload. Stop when pos equals End. Calling again with End
        restarts traversal. Do not mutate the list while retaining
        positional iteration state.

    RESULT
        Always TRUE. Read pos to detect exhaustion; it is not a BOOL has-
        next result.

    SEE ALSO
        MUIM_NList_Select, MUIM_NList_GetEntry, MUIM_NList_NextSelected

NList.mcc/MUIM_NList_Redraw

    NAME
        MUIM_NList_Redraw -- Invalidate display data and request redraw.

    SYNOPSIS
        DoMethod(obj, MUIM_NList_Redraw, pos);

    INPUTS
        pos -- Display-row index or MUIV_NList_Redraw_Active (-1), All (-2),
        Title (-3), Selected (-4), VisibleCols (-5).

    FUNCTION
        Clear display/parse caches and process the requested scope. All
        requests parsing, column, scrollbar and wrapping updates as well as
        redraw; Title updates title state. Selected uses line flags or the
        character-range bounds. Some per-row/column processing occurs only
        while drawing is enabled. Invalid row indices perform no row redraw.

        Nested drawing can queue the operation on the application.
        Completion is therefore not synchronous. MUIM_List_Redraw shares the
        handler, but its All request is rewritten to an internal deferred
        path; that message must be writable. Use the NList method for its
        documented scope.

    RESULT
        Always TRUE, including no-op or deferred requests. It is not proof
        that pixels were drawn.

    SEE ALSO
        MUIM_NList_RedrawEntry

NList.mcc/MUIM_NList_RedrawEntry

    NAME
        MUIM_NList_RedrawEntry -- Request redraw for matching stored payload
        pointers.

    SYNOPSIS
        DoMethod(obj, MUIM_NList_RedrawEntry, entry);

    INPUTS
        entry -- Non-NULL borrowed stored payload pointer.

    FUNCTION
        Scan all display records for pointer identity, invalidate pixel
        lengths for matches and request redraw. Multiple records with the
        same pointer are all considered. The method does not copy, replace
        or destroy payloads, and does not normalize each wrapped row to an
        owner before comparison.

    RESULT
        TRUE if a record matched; FALSE for NULL or no match. TRUE does not
        prove immediate rendering.

    SEE ALSO
        MUIM_NList_Redraw, MUIM_NList_GetPos

NList.mcc/MUIM_NList_Remove

    NAME
        MUIM_NList_Remove -- Remove one logical entry or selected entries.

    SYNOPSIS
        DoMethod(obj, MUIM_NList_Remove, pos);

    INPUTS
        pos -- Zero-based display index, or MUIV_NList_Remove_First (0),
        Active (-1), Last (-2), Selected (-3).

    FUNCTION
        Normalize a wrapped continuation to its logical owner. Destruct the
        removed payload, update row positions and selection/active state,
        and request redraw and any needed rewrapping. Selected scans entry
        selection flags; it is not a text-range deletion operation.

        Removing an active entry keeps the numerical active position for a
        following entry when available and clamps it at the end. Removing an
        earlier entry shifts the active index down. The implementation may
        select an adjacent entry while maintaining selection state. Removal
        of the final entry uses Clear. Retained indices and list-owned
        payload pointers may become invalid.

    RESULT
        TRUE when removal proceeds, FALSE for no valid target or no selected
        entries. Destructor failures are not reported.

    SEE ALSO
        MUIM_NList_Clear, MUIM_NList_Destruct

NList.mcc/MUIM_NList_ReplaceSingle

    NAME
        MUIM_NList_ReplaceSingle -- Replace a logical payload at an existing
        position.

    SYNOPSIS
        DoMethod(obj, MUIM_NList_ReplaceSingle, entry, pos, wrapcol, align);

    INPUTS
        entry -- Non-NULL replacement input.

        pos -- Zero-based display index or Insert_Top, Insert_Active or
        Insert_Bottom (MUIV_NList_ prefix). Active must exist. Sorted and
        other invalid positions fail; they do not append.

        wrapcol -- NOWRAP or one of WRAPCOL0 through WRAPCOL6. Only one
        display column can wrap; the implementation masks to these bits and
        selects the lowest set bit if several are supplied.

        align -- ALIGN_LEFT, ALIGN_CENTER, ALIGN_RIGHT or ALIGN_JUSTIFY.
        Stored after masking with 0x0700. Alignment escape sequences in the
        parsed display text can override this initial style.

    FUNCTION
        Normalize a wrapped continuation to its owner. Construct the new
        payload before destructing the old payload and updating the existing
        record. Set InsertPosition, clear character selection and mark the
        list unsorted. Do not pass an alias that becomes invalid when the
        old payload is destructed.

        If Construct returns NULL, this implementation removes the old
        entry. Replacement is therefore not transactional and does not
        preserve the old value on construction failure.

    RESULT
        TRUE after replacement, or the removal result after construction
        failure. Thus TRUE can mean the old entry was removed without
        replacement. NULL input or an invalid position returns FALSE.

    SEE ALSO
        MUIM_NList_Construct, MUIM_NList_Destruct, MUIM_NList_Remove

NList.mcc/MUIM_NList_Select

    NAME
        MUIM_NList_Select -- Change or query selection.

    SYNOPSIS
        DoMethod(obj, MUIM_NList_Select, pos, seltype, state);

    INPUTS
        pos -- Display-row index, MUIV_NList_Select_Active (-1), or All
        (-2). seltype -- MUIV_NList_Select_Off (0), On (1), Toggle (2), Ask
        (3). state -- Optional writable LONG pointer; initialize it before
        calling.

    FUNCTION
        In line mode, On/Off/Toggle require effective multiselection; Ask
        can query one row without it. All with multiselection returns a
        count rather than one row's state. Under the list-selection
        preference, All/Off or All/Toggle can retain the active row. With
        Input FALSE the helper returns TRUE without updating state. Invalid
        single targets return FALSE. MultiTestHook can reject On while the
        output still reports the requested state or attempted count; Ask
        reads the stored flags.

        In character mode, Off clears the whole range regardless of pos. On
        selects whole display rows: All selects the entire list, otherwise
        the resolved position is clamped to the list. Ask writes the
        covered-row span and ignores pos. Toggle has no implementation but
        returns TRUE. Only Ask writes state in this mode. The endpoint
        calculation is not a reliable empty-selection test for uninitialized
        or invalidated ranges.

    RESULT
        Path-specific BOOL. TRUE does not guarantee a change or a written
        state value. Line Ask returns On/Off through state for one row; All
        returns a count when supported. Character Ask returns a span count.

    SEE ALSO
        MUIA_NList_TypeSelect, MUIA_NList_MultiTestHook,
        MUIM_NList_NextSelected

NList.mcc/MUIM_NList_SelectChange

    NAME
        MUIM_NList_SelectChange -- Handle an internal selection or active-
        position callback.

    SYNOPSIS
        Subclass handler: struct MUIP_NList_SelectChange *msg;

    INPUTS
        pos -- Affected display row, or Active_Off for a cleared active
        position. state -- Select_On, Select_Off or Select_Active, with the
        MUIV_NList_ prefix. flags -- May include
        MUIV_NList_SelectChange_Flag_Multi.

    FUNCTION
        Override this callback in an NList subclass to observe the changes
        emitted by internal selection helpers. Do not call it to change
        selection; the default dispatcher performs no action.

        The Multi bit identifies the helper path that also marks changed
        rows. Programmatic Select calls use that path too, so this bit does
        not prove the mouse button was held. On/Off callbacks arise from
        line-selection helpers; active-position callbacks have no TypeSelect
        guard and can occur while selection mode changes. It is not
        exclusively a line-mode callback in every case.

    RESULT
        Zero from the default dispatcher. Selection helpers ignore the
        callback result.

    SEE ALSO
        MUIA_NList_SelectChange, MUIM_NList_Select

NList.mcc/MUIM_NList_SetActive

    NAME
        MUIM_NList_SetActive -- Set an active row by index or payload
        pointer.

    SYNOPSIS
        DoMethod(obj, MUIM_NList_SetActive, pos, flags);

    INPUTS
        pos -- SIPTR index/Active selector, or payload pointer when Entry is
        set. flags -- ULONG, zero or a combination of
        MUIV_NList_SetActive_Entry (1) and Jump_Center (2).

    FUNCTION
        Entry searches for the first stored pointer identical to pos. It
        does not compare content or the original input pointer when
        Construct returned a different payload. A failed lookup becomes
        Active_Off; it can clear the current active entry.

        In ordinary line mode, activation also brings the row into view.
        Jump_Center requests centering when activation proceeds. Repeating
        the current active row normally skips the entire update, including
        centering; use Jump_Active_Center for an independent centering
        request.

        In character mode or with Input FALSE, the separate navigation path
        does not establish a normal active row. Negative selectors passed
        through this method can be interpreted as First selectors. Do not
        assume equivalence with the attribute setter in these modes.

    RESULT
        The activation helper result. TRUE can mean clearing Active or
        navigation work, not necessarily selecting the requested payload;
        FALSE can mean the same row was already active.

    SEE ALSO
        MUIA_NList_Active, MUIM_NList_GetPos, MUIM_NList_Jump

NList.mcc/MUIM_NList_SetColumnCol

    NAME
        MUIM_NList_SetColumnCol --

    SYNOPSIS
        DoMethod(obj,MUIM_NList_SetColumnCol,LONG column,LONG col);

    FUNCTION
        set which displayhook col is at the visible column.

    INPUTS
        column  number of the column (visible).
                if MUIV_NList_SetColumnCol_Default then the given (diplayhook) col
                will come back to its default/original (from List_Format) position.

        col     col number (displayhook one).
                if MUIV_NList_SetColumnCol_Default then the given visible column
                will come back to its default/original (from List_Format) position.


                both set to MUIV_NList_SetColumnCol_Default make all columns
                come back to their default/original (from List_Format) position.

    RESULT
        None.

    NOTE
        MUIM_NList_SetColumnCol always exchange the moved column with the column
        which was were it move.

    SEE ALSO
        MUIM_NList_ColToColumn, MUIM_NList_ColumnToCol, MUIA_NList_Columns
NList.mcc/MUIM_NList_Sort

    NAME
        MUIM_NList_Sort -- Sort logical entries using the current comparison
        policy.

    SYNOPSIS
        DoMethod(obj, MUIM_NList_Sort);

    FUNCTION
        Compare payloads through MUIM_NList_Compare and the current sort
        types. Wrapped rows are compared through their owners; original
        positions break equal comparisons, preserving their relative order.
        Reorder existing records without constructing or destructing
        payloads. The active entry and tracked InsertPosition follow
        reordered records.

        The default comparator uses Stricmp and requires strings. Custom
        payloads need a suitable comparator. Required wrapping, selection
        and redraw work may still occur when the order does not change.

    RESULT
        TRUE if the sort exchanged entries; FALSE if no exchanges occurred
        or the temporary sort array could not be allocated. FALSE is not an
        unambiguous failure report.

    SEE ALSO
        MUIM_NList_Compare, MUIM_NList_Sort2, MUIM_NList_Sort3

NList.mcc/MUIM_NList_Sort2

    NAME
        MUIM_NList_Sort2 -- Update the primary sort value and sort.

    SYNOPSIS
        DoMethod(obj, MUIM_NList_Sort2, sort_type, sort_type_add);

    INPUTS
        sort_type, sort_type_add -- LONG policy value and update amount.

    FUNCTION
        If sort_type_add is nonzero and (current &
        ~MUIV_NList_SortTypeAdd_Mask) equals sort_type, add sort_type_add to
        current. Otherwise assign sort_type. The mask is 0xC0000000. The
        header provides SortTypeAdd_None (0), SortTypeAdd_2Values
        (0x80000000), and SortTypeAdd_4Values (0x40000000), each with the
        MUIV_NList_ prefix. The comparison policy interprets these values;
        the class does not itself select a column or reverse Stricmp.

        Update SortType, set its attribute, then call Sort. SortType2 is
        unchanged. A custom Compare implementation or CompareHook2 can
        interpret the updated value; the default comparator ignores it.

    RESULT
        The Sort change indicator. FALSE does not mean the sort attribute
        was unchanged or rejected.

    SEE ALSO
        MUIM_NList_Sort, MUIM_NList_Sort3, MUIA_NList_SortType

NList.mcc/MUIM_NList_Sort3

    NAME
        MUIM_NList_Sort3 -- Update one or both sort values and sort.

    SYNOPSIS
        DoMethod(obj, MUIM_NList_Sort3, sort_type, sort_type_add, which);

    INPUTS
        sort_type and sort_type_add -- As for Sort2. which --
        MUIV_NList_Sort3_SortType_1 (1), SortType_2 (2) or SortType_Both
        (0).

    FUNCTION
        Apply the Sort2 update rule to the selected value. Selector 2
        updates only SortType2; selector 1 updates only SortType. Both
        updates SortType first and copies the resulting value to SortType2.
        Unrecognized selectors follow the Both branch; use the declared
        constants.

        Set the affected attributes, then sort with the resulting policy
        values. A zero change result does not undo those updates.

    RESULT
        The Sort change indicator.

    SEE ALSO
        MUIM_NList_Sort, MUIM_NList_Sort2, MUIA_NList_SortType2

NList.mcc/MUIM_NList_TestPos

    NAME
        MUIM_NList_TestPos -- Hit-test displayed list geometry.

    SYNOPSIS
        DoMethod(obj, MUIM_NList_TestPos, x, y, &res);

    INPUTS
        x, y -- Window-relative coordinates for a laid-out, displayed list.
        Both MUI_MAXMAX use the stored last click; x=MUI_MAXMAX and y=0 use
        the stored mouse position. res -- Initialized struct
        MUI_NList_TestPos_Result.

    FUNCTION
        Return a display-row index, visible-column index, region flags and
        offsets. entry is -1 without a valid row; column may identify a
        title column even then. Flags describe ABOVE, BELOW, LEFT, RIGHT,
        BAR, TITLE and ONTOP regions. xoffset is relative to the column;
        yoffset is relative to the row center. Character fields refer to
        parsed display text, including preparse text, not necessarily raw
        payload bytes.

        Preset char_number to -2 to skip character lookup. The later
        preparse adjustment can change this sentinel and preparse even when
        lookup was skipped; do not rely on the old promise that -2 survives.
        Set it again before each skipped lookup and ignore the
        character/preparse outputs from that call.

        Require valid layout and a nonzero line height; this method does not
        validate setup. MUIM_List_TestPos instead requires the smaller
        MUI_List_TestPos_Result and uses a separate handler without these
        stored-coordinate shortcuts.

    RESULT
        TRUE for non-NULL output, including positions outside entries. FALSE
        for NULL output. Inspect entry, column and flags to determine the
        hit.

    SEE ALSO
        MUIM_NList_GetEntry, MUIM_NList_GetEntryInfo

NList.mcc/MUIM_NList_UseImage

    NAME
        MUIM_NList_UseImage --

    SYNOPSIS
        DoMethod(obj,MUIM_NList_UseImage,Object *obj, ULONG imgnum, ULONG flags);

    FUNCTION
        If you want to use MUIM_NList_CreateImage/MUIM_NList_DeleteImage
        as you should, you must make a NList subclass which call them
        from Setup() and Cleanup(), and it's sometime complicated.

        To avoid that, you can use MUIM_NList_UseImage. NList will
        store the Bitmap/Bodychunk object you give and will make
        CreateImage and DeleteImage itself !

        MUIM_NList_UseImage can use same object as MUIM_NList_CreateImage !

        NULL is a valid obj. It will erase any previously UseImage
        with the same imgnum.

        The imgnum you give is the number that you will use in
        \33o[<n>] sequence as the <n> number.
        MUIM_NList_UseImage will accept 0 <= imgnum < 8192, anyway
        use small value if you can because an array will be allocated
        with the biggest imgnum value as size to store the
        Bitmap/Bodychunk objects.

        DoMethod(obj,MUIM_NList_UseImage, NULL, MUIV_NList_UseImage_All, 0)
        will set NULL to all stored objects, so you'll be able to dispose your
        Bitmap/Bodychunk objects if you want, without waiting the NList
        objet dispose.

        flags is the same than for MUIM_NList_CreateImage (0 unless special case).

    RESULT
        TRUE if succeeded to store the obj (and allocate the array
        if needed), else FALSE.

        There is no way to know if the MUIM_NList_CreateImage
        needed to draw will succeed/has succeeded.

        ATTENTION:
        The given Bitmap/Bodychunk object MUST be valid until the
        NList object is disposed or you set another object (or NULL)
        at the same imgnum !
        The Bitmap/Bodychunk object can be shared with other NList
        object because NList just use it to get informations, anyway
        you mustn't change informations of that object. If you to do
        so, do a UseImage,NULL,x , change it then do UseImage,imgobj,x
        again.

    SEE ALSO
        MUIM_NList_CreateImage, MUIM_NList_DeleteImage

